I cannot believe that the majority of  Americans want to finance the detention  of innocent people hi i'm anthony fisher  with recent TV and I'm joined by Hena  Shamsi the director of the National  Security Project with the American Civil  Liberties Union and one of the lawyers  representing Mamadou ruled slaw he the  author of the Guantanamo diary and a  current inmate at Guantanamo Bay thanks  so much for joining us it's a pleasure  to be with you can you tell us a little  bit about slaw he and what his  background was and how he ended up in US  custody absolutely  so Muhammadu is a 44 year old more  Italian citizen he was born there he won  a scholarship to Germany and went and  studied engineering in Germany he'd  returned to Mauritania in around 2000  and voluntarily turned himself in to  questioning by the Mauritania and police  and that would be the last his family  saw of him over the next 14 years he was  transferred to Jordan where he was  tortured and abused transferred again by  the United States to Bagram where he was  further tortured and abused and then  transferred to Guantanamo in August 2002  where he was subjected to a special  interrogation program that terrible  euphemism we've come to now know is  torture  authorized specifically by Donald  Rumsfeld he remains there still why was  the United States looking for him after  9/11 the United States  remember at this period of time the  United States was picking up people in  around the world and capturing them  sending some to black sites sending  others to Guantanamo and they picked him  up based on a variety of suspicions  which have all been shown to be false so  they alleged involvement in the  Millennium plot turned out not to be  true alleged millennium plan which was  to attack the Los Angeles Airport that's  right  that's turned out not to be true they  alleged that he was a recruiter for  al-qaeda  that's turned out not to be true and I  think it's important to point out that  the only neutral person who has  evaluated all the facts  case is a federal judge who in 2010  ordered Mohammad who released but the  government appealed and he still remains  at Guantanamo but he did swear  allegiance to al-qaeda in early 90s and  went to Afghanistan to fight the Soviets  correct correct and he admits that he's  admitted that to the military over and  over again and what he said and what the  judge found was that he went like so  many others to Afghanistan to fight the  communist regime there in the early 90s  he was part of a movement that was at  the time supported by the United States  and the al Qaeda that he joined was very  different and the judge said this from  what al Qaeda later became he had no  connection with them post 1992 no there  was no evidence of any threats of  violence or religious extremism that  would have tied him to what we know of  his bin Laden's al Qaeda no and he's  repeatedly rejected that he describes  that also in his book so there is  actually no evidence of a legal basis to  detain him he's never been part of the  armed conflict between the United States  and al Qaeda and he's never participated  in any hostilities against the United  States let's talk a little bit about the  book he wrote the book in 2005 and now  it's 2015 and it's being released to  wide acclaim can you talk a little bit  about how he came to write the book and  how it was able to get out and see the  light of day well I'll start with an  anecdote which I think gives you a  flavor of who Muhammadu is which is that  when of one of our co-counsel asked him  to talk about the interrogations that he  had gone through to describe all of the  interrogations his response was are you  crazy  that's like asking Charlie Sheen how  many women he stated but in around the  summer and fall of 2005 he started  writing down because he wanted the world  to know and he specifically wanted  Americans to know his story this is a  book that's very much addressed to all  of us it turned into a 400 plus page  manuscript and we his lawyers fought for  over six years through litigation and  negotiation with the government to get  it cleared for release it was cleared  with over 2,500 redactions those  reductions are still in the book as  published but it is I think and I hope  people will read it and find a deeply  humane human and sometimes even funny  account of one man's journey through the  the arduous place that the United States  took him and I think another part of  this that's so unusual is that Muhammad  whose account is of being in a place  that was established to demian humanize  people like him and he sets out in his  book to describe what that's like but he  also humanizes those who are supposed to  represent us his American jailers some  of them his tortures and interrogators  and you get a real sense of what it is  that we have done not only to the  victims of our policies but what it is  that we do to our military service  members when we ask them tera Gators to  break the minds and bodies of human  beings can you talk a little bit about  that how what's an example in the book  of him trying to humanize his captors he  tells he tries to understand why his  interrogators are are asking him these  questions he talks about his guards with  some of the guards he the friendlier  guards he engages in discussions about  politics about religion about chess some  of those guards become his friends they  give him books when they are leaving off  rotation hoping that he's going to  someday be released and I think that  that humanity comes through he talks  about how this wasn't a family that he  chose but because of the circumstances  he's in some of these people are the  family that he now has even as he yearns  for the family that he has not seen for  over 14 years for a time the US  government was claiming that slaw he  would bro  and was one of their greatest sources of  information on al-qaeda and now it  doesn't appear that much of the  information he gave is useful at all can  you talk a little bit about why the u.s.  is positioned on the information he gave  has changed well I can tell you what  Muhammadu says in the book about the  information that he was providing to the  government which is that he was  subjected to terrible torture beatings  stress positions extreme temperatures  faked execution threats against his  mother's and after a period of that he  started to self-incriminating  he said I told them you know the worst  about myself because that's what they  wanted to hear and so you get a real  sense about the only two things that  torture guarantees which is false  information and pain  there's one part of the book that that  is particularly striking in that way he  says that  he learned to forget the words I don't  know because when he thought about those  words he grew nauseous what stops llahi  from petitioning for habeas for habeas  corpus well Mamadou did petition for  habeas corpus and that's where we  represented him in court and that's  where you know he's still waiting for  his case to go forward one key aspect of  this is he like so many of the other men  at Montana mo is being held without  charge or trial and there are two  military prosecutors former military  prosecutors who refused to go forward on  his case one of them Colonel Stuart  couch found out about the torture and  decided that as a man of religion ethics  and morals he could no longer continue  on Mohammed his case the second is the  former chief military prosecutor at  Guantanamo Colonel Morris Davis who said  that he tried to look for any crime with  which to charge Mohammed doing the  military commissions and couldn't find  one can you talk a little bit about  slacking now he's aware that the book  has been published and that it's gotten  a positive reception  what's weird what is his life like now  and what's next for him well because of  the government secrecy regime at  Guantanamo I can't describe to you what  life is like for him what I can tell you  is that he's in a prison he's in a  terrible place he was recently visited  by one of our my co-counsel who told him  that his book had become a New York  Times bestseller and was on the  bestseller list for three weeks about  the close to 50,000 people who signed a  petition that by the ACLU for his  release and he was deeply deeply moved  he is tired he's been through a lot he's  never been charged with a crime he wants  to go home this book is really addressed  to the American public and at the end of  the book Mohammed who says I want to  know what the public thinks I cannot  believe that the majority of Americans  want to finance the detention of  innocent people and this book is his  hope that people of conscience people  who are committed to Liberty's will read  it be moved by it and be moved to do  something about it  what do you what do you say to people  who think that indefinite detention  without charges is in the national  security interests definite detention  without charge or trial is deeply  American American goes against our laws  and our values but let's take on that  argument which is that there are a tiny  tiny fraction of people released from  Guantanamo all of them released by the  Bush administration who have been  accused of being hostile to or engaging  in acts against Americans Guantanamo is  very existence right is according to the  president national security experts  military and intelligence a national  security threat to us the fact that it  exists hurts our national security it  also hurts our finances and economies  the current estimated cost by Miami  Herald of holding a single prisoner at  Guantanamo for a year is over three  million dollars now  so when it hurts our laws our values our  morality our ethics there is no good  argument to keep it in place and it  should be closed we've been talking with  henna Shamsi the director of the  National Security Project with the  American Civil Liberties Union and one  of the lawyers representing Mamadou  ruled Salahi the author of the  Guantanamo diary thanks so much for  joining us for recent TV I'm Anthony  Fisher
 

